{"causes": ["The sustained military pressure from the expanding Sena dynasty under Vijayasena significantly weakened the territorial integrity of the Pala Empire.", "The internal fragmentation and waning central authority within the Pala Empire prior to Gopala IV's reign created vulnerabilities for external aggression.", "The gradual decline of Buddhist patronage and the rise of Hindu revivalism contributed to a shift in the political and cultural landscape of Bengal."], "effects": ["The continued territorial losses under Gopala IV accelerated the decline of the Pala Empire, paving the way for Sena supremacy in Bengal.", "The *Pancharaksha* manuscript, dated to his reign, became a crucial primary source for understanding Pala-era art and religious practices in the 12th century.", "Gopala IV's reign marked a definitive transition period, solidifying the shift of political power from the Buddhist Palas to the Hindu Senas in eastern India."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "kumarapala", "sourceName": "Kumarapala", "verb": "SUCCEEDS", "targetSlug": "gopala-iii", "targetName": "Gopala III", "context": "Gopala IV (Gopala III) succeeded his father, Kumarapala, as the Pala monarch around 1129 CE."}, {"sourceSlug": "gopala-iii", "sourceName": "Gopala III", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "bengal", "targetName": "Bengal", "context": "Gopala IV's reign as Pala king was primarily centered in the Bengal region of the Indian subcontinent from approximately 1129 to 1144 CE."}, {"sourceSlug": "vijayasena", "sourceName": "Vijayasena", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "gopala-iii", "targetName": "Gopala III", "context": "The military expansion of Vijayasena's Sena dynasty significantly challenged and reduced the territories controlled by Gopala IV during his reign."}, {"sourceSlug": "gopala-iii", "sourceName": "Gopala III", "verb": "TRANSMITS", "targetSlug": "buddhist-art-and-literature", "targetName": "Buddhist Art and Literature", "context": "The *Pancharaksha* manuscript, created during Gopala IV's 15th regnal year, exemplifies the continued patronage and transmission of Buddhist artistic and literary traditions."}, {"sourceSlug": "pala-empire", "sourceName": "Pala Empire", "verb": "CONTAINS", "targetSlug": "gopala-iii", "targetName": "Gopala III", "context": "Gopala IV was the nineteenth ruler of the Pala Empire, presiding over its declining territories in the 12th century."}], "places": [{"name": "Bengal, India", "role": "Primary Reign Region"}, {"name": "British Library, United Kingdom", "role": "Manuscript Repository"}, {"name": "Indian Subcontinent, Asia", "role": "Geographic Context"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Gopala III", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "gopala-iii"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q12424502", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gopala_IV"], "tags": ["Monarchs & Rulers", "political-systems", "empire-and-colonialism", "geopolitical-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 4, "significanceNarrative": "Gopala IV's reign represents a late stage in the Pala Empire's decline, notable for the final documented evidence of Pala rule through a significant manuscript and for its role in the transition of power to the Sena dynasty in Bengal.", "significanceCategory": "local"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-08-19T10:11:16Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#32241284457", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Gopala IV previously called Gopala III, was the successor and son to the Pala king Kumarapala in the Bengal region of the Indian subcontinent, and nineteenth ruler of the Pala line reigning for at least 15 years, proved by a manuscript currently at British library.", "newValue": "Gopala IV, initially identified as Gopala III in earlier historical accounts, ascended to the throne of the Pala Empire in the Bengal region of the Indian subcontinent around 1129 CE, succeeding his father, King Kumarapala. His reign, which lasted until his death in 1144 CE, marked a period of continued contraction for the once-dominant Pala dynasty, as various regional powers, most notably the resurgent Sena dynasty, intensified their territorial encroachments and challenged Pala suzerainty. Despite the diminishing territorial control, Gopala IV's rule is evidenced by significant epigraphic and manuscript discoveries, providing valuable insights into the political and cultural landscape of 12th-century Bengal.

During his fifteen-year tenure, Gopala IV struggled to maintain the integrity of the Pala realm against the rising power of the Senas under Vijayasena, who systematically annexed territories in eastern Bengal. While specific military campaigns or grand architectural projects attributed directly to Gopala IV are scarce, his reign is notably documented by a manuscript of the *Pancharaksha* text, dated to the 15th regnal year (c. 1144 CE), which is now preserved in the British Library. This manuscript, inscribed during his rule, underscores the continued patronage of Buddhist scholarship and art, even as the political fortunes of the Pala kings waned.

The legacy of Gopala IV is primarily understood as part of the twilight of the Pala Empire, a once-mighty Buddhist kingdom that had shaped the cultural and political contours of Bengal and Bihar for centuries. His inability to halt the territorial losses set the stage for the eventual dissolution of Pala authority, paving the way for the complete ascendancy of the Sena dynasty across most of Bengal. This transition profoundly impacted the region's socio-political structure, shifting from a Buddhist-patronizing empire to a Hindu-centric kingdom, altering the trajectory of local religious and artistic patronage."}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-19T10:11:16Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#32241284457", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "Classical"}, {"title": "Dynastic succession and sovereign authority traditions", "type": "Idea", "year": ""}], "newValue": ["The sustained military pressure from the expanding Sena dynasty under Vijayasena significantly weakened the territorial integrity of the Pala Empire.", "The internal fragmentation and waning central authority within the Pala Empire prior to Gopala IV's reign created vulnerabilities for external aggression.", "The gradual decline of Buddhist patronage and the rise of Hindu revivalism contributed to a shift in the political and cultural landscape of Bengal."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-19T10:11:16Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#32241284457", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Established dynastic legacies and territorial boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "1144"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1144"}], "newValue": ["The continued territorial losses under Gopala IV accelerated the decline of the Pala Empire, paving the way for Se